talk(181,'1970','','P000294','1970',r'The catalogue year heading introduces the February 1970 entry. It carries no separate mathematical assertion and does not begin a new academic-year chapter. The preceding Hewitt leaf remains part of the 1969--1970 academic year.',kind='heading')
talk(182,'1970-02-23','D. A. Kazhdan','P000294-P000295','Griffiths: monodromy and regularity of periods',r'Kazhdan explains the cohomology local system of an algebraic family over a punctured curve, its Gauss--Manin connection, the monodromy theorem and regular-singular expansions of periods. The parenthetical assertion that monodromy itself is unipotent needs a finite-base-change qualification. Griffiths\textquotesingle s published survey records an August 1968 invited lecture and submission in September 1969, although its print issue is March 1970.')
item(182,'P000294','theorem','Quasi-unipotence and the monodromy exponent',r'For monodromy \(T:H^i(V_s)\to H^i(V_s)\), the notes state \((T^N-1)^{i+1}=0\) for some \(N\), then call \(T\) unipotent.',r'For a smooth projective family of complex algebraic varieties over a punctured smooth algebraic curve, local monodromy on \(H^i\) has roots of unity as eigenvalues, and for some positive integer \(N\), \((T^N-I)^{i+1}=0\). Thus \(T\) is quasi-unipotent; after the base change \(z=w^N\), monodromy is unipotent with nilpotency index at most \(i+1\).',r'\citet[Theorem 3.1, pp.~235--236]{griffiths1970periods} states this theorem and discusses the proofs of Landman, Clemens and Grothendieck. The survey was submitted 18 September 1969 and published March 1970, after an invited lecture on 29 August 1968; the result predates the February 1970 seminar report.',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='Smoothness and projectivity over the punctured curve are sufficient modern hypotheses. The correction concerns the separate parenthesis, not the correctly written power of monodromy.')
item(182,'P000294','construction','The cohomology local system and Gauss--Manin bundle',r'The bundle \(W\to S\) has algebraic-curve base; \(\pi_1(S)\) acts on fibre cohomology. The associated analytic vector bundle is trivial with parallel transport on the universal cover.',r'For a smooth proper morphism \(\pi:W\to S\) of complex algebraic varieties, \(\mathcal L=R^i\pi_*\mathbb C\) is a finite-rank local system. Its analytic bundle \(E=\mathcal L\otimes_{\mathbb C}\mathcal O_S\) has the canonical flat Gauss--Manin connection, with horizontal sections \(\mathcal L\). Pullback to a simply connected covering trivializes the local system and its flat bundle.',r'This is the classical construction recalled in \citet[Section 2, pp.~231--233]{griffiths1970periods}. The notebook\textquotesingle s phrase algebraic bundle is replaced by an explicit smooth proper family, not an arbitrary algebraic map.',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(182,'P000294','method','Picard--Fuchs equations in a local frame',r'The first abbreviated line reads \(\partial/\partial z=A(z)\); the next complete line says every flat section satisfies \(f^{\prime}(z)=A(z)f(z)\), and conversely.',r'In a local frame of a flat holomorphic vector bundle on a complex curve, a horizontal section with coefficient column \(u(z)\) satisfies a first-order system \(u^{\prime}(z)=A(z)u(z)\), with the sign determined by the connection-matrix convention. Conversely every solution determines a horizontal section. Changes of frame transform the coefficient matrix by the usual gauge formula.',r'The local connection equation is standard and is used explicitly in \citet[Section 4]{griffiths1970periods}. The complete equation in the notebook resolves the earlier operator abbreviation.',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The vector of coefficients and the connection sign convention are specified; the abbreviated first line is not treated as an equality of an operator with a matrix.')
item(182,'P000294-P000295','construction','Periods pair moving cycles with cohomology',r'The notes transport a cycle \(\phi_z\subset V_z\) and write \((f_i(z),\phi_z)\), described as the value of cohomology on homology and a vector function.',r'A locally constant family of homology classes \(\gamma_z\in H_i(V_z,\mathbb C)\) is horizontal for the dual Gauss--Manin connection. For a local cohomology frame \(\omega_j(z)\), the period functions \(\langle\omega_j(z),\gamma_z\rangle\) satisfy the induced dual differential system. They are not individually constant unless the corresponding cohomology classes also are horizontal.',r'The homology--cohomology pairing and period system are the construction in \citet[Sections 2 and 4]{griffiths1970periods}. This identifies the recorded cycle example while keeping the bundle and its dual distinct.',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The flat cycle lies in the homology local system; period coordinates are obtained by pairing with the chosen cohomology frame.')
item(182,'P000295','theorem','Regular singularity of the Gauss--Manin connection',r'The theorem attributed to Griffiths says that a basis can be chosen with \(zA(z)\) holomorphic at the puncture.',r'For the Gauss--Manin connection of a smooth projective algebraic family over a punctured curve, there is a logarithmic extension across each puncture: in a local holomorphic frame of that extension, the connection matrix has at most a simple pole. Equivalently, starting with an algebraic meromorphic frame, the system is regular singular. An arbitrary holomorphic gauge on the punctured disk can introduce worse apparent growth.',r'\citet[Theorem 4.3, p.~238]{griffiths1970periods} states regularity and explains its relation to the monodromy theorem. The article was submitted in September 1969; publication in March 1970 does not make this a post-seminar discovery.',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The basis is a frame of an extension, or an admissible meromorphic frame; the assertion is not invariant under unrestricted holomorphic gauges on a punctured disk.')
item(182,'P000295','theorem','Puiseux--logarithmic local expansions',r'The displayed flat-section series has fractional powers \(z^{n/N}\) and finitely many powers of \(\log z\); its lower-index handwriting is uncertain and is transcribed as \(n\leq0\).',r'After choosing a branch on a sector and a meromorphic frame, regular singularity with quasi-unipotent monodromy gives a convergent expansion \(\sum_{n\geq n_0}z^{n/N}\sum_{j=0}^{i}c_{nj}(\log z)^j\) for horizontal degree-\(i\) sections, for some integer \(n_0\) and positive integer \(N\). The coefficients are vectors. There are only finitely many negative powers after ramification, and logarithmic degree is bounded by the monodromy nilpotency index minus one.',r'The factorization of a fundamental solution into a meromorphic factor and a monodromy exponential is described by \citet[pp.~238--239]{griffiths1970periods}; it gives exactly this expansion under the stated hypotheses. The index is mathematically reconstructed rather than claimed as a certain decipherment.',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The finite principal part, sector, coefficient vectors and direction of summation are explicit additions needed for a correct local expansion.')
item(182,'P000295','theorem','Moderate growth of periods near a puncture',r'The lemma bounds any flat section by \(C|z|^{-a}|\log z|^b\).',r'In a meromorphic frame of the regular-singular Gauss--Manin bundle, on each sufficiently small sector with a fixed bounded range of argument, every horizontal section satisfies \(\|u(z)\|\leq C|z|^{-a}(1+|\log z|)^b\), for suitable constants depending on the section and sector. A freely chosen holomorphic frame with an essential singularity would invalidate this statement.',r'The moderate-growth characterization is explained in \citet[pp.~238--239]{griffiths1970periods}, and follows directly from the preceding Puiseux--log expansion. This is a theorem in the specified geometric and gauge category, not a universal estimate for flat analytic connections.',refs=['griffiths1970periods'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
correction('P000294','monodromy parenthesis',r'\(T\) is unipotent',r'\(T\) is quasi-unipotent; \(T^N\) is unipotent','Both the Russian scan and the transcription have T without the exponent in the parenthesis. The preceding displayed formula gives the correct qualified assertion.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000295','flat-section expansion lower index',r'\(\sum_{n\leq0}\)',r'For the regular-singular expansion use \(\sum_{n\geq n_0}\) after finite ramification','The scan has crowded index strokes and the transcription explicitly marks the reading as uncertain. Mathematics requires a finite principal part; the proposed index is a reconstruction, not a definitive paleographic correction.','both','ambiguity')
checkpoint()

talk(186,'1970-09-07','','P000296','Programme for the 1970--1971 seminar',r'The opening programme assigns Kazhdan the relation between formal and algebraic geometry, Arnold work of Griffiths, Mityagin and Henkin their own work, and Moishezon work of Artin. It also lists finite-group \(K\)-theory with Brown, Tate, Thompson, Swan and Katz, Hironaka, and for the next semester zeta functions, \(p\)-adic harmonic analysis and finite groups. These are proposed topics and names, not stated theorems or open conjectures. The programme physically precedes the Kazhdan lecture on the same leaf.',kind='plan')
talk(184,'1970-09-07','D. A. Kazhdan','P000296-P000299','Foundations of schemes and quasi-coherent sheaves',r'Kazhdan begins an expository course on coherent-sheaf cohomology with spectra, localization, sheaves, fibre products and quasi-coherent modules. A Kirillov intervention explains the gluing construction of products. The source uses the older distinction between preschemes and separated schemes; the modern terminology is made explicit. The entry ends at the dated 28 September heading on P000299, rather than including that later lecture.')
item(184,'P000296','definition','Affine spectra as ringed spaces',r'A scheme is introduced as a topological space with a structure sheaf of rings; for an affine scheme, its points are prime ideals of a commutative ring with identity.',r'For a commutative unital ring \(A\), \(\operatorname{Spec}A\) consists of its proper prime ideals. Equipped with the Zariski topology and structure sheaf, it is an affine scheme; its stalk at \(\mathfrak p\) is the local ring \(A_{\mathfrak p}\). The zero ring has empty spectrum. A scheme is a locally ringed space covered by such affine models.',r'This is the established Grothendieck framework of \citet{grothendieck1960ega1}; its current formulation is checked in \citet[Section 26.5]{stacks2026foundations}. It continues the seminar foundations of 1965-85.1 and 1968-163.1--2.',refs=['grothendieck1960ega1','stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(184,'P000296-P000297','problem','Zariski topology and principal-open basis',r'For an ideal \(I\), \(U_I\) comprises primes not containing \(I\). The exercises ask that these sets form a topology and that the sets \(U_f\) form a basis.',r'With \(D(I)=\{\mathfrak p:I\not\subseteq\mathfrak p\}\), one has \(D(I)=\bigcup_{f\in I}D(f)\), \(D(f)\cap D(g)=D(fg)\), \(D(0)=\varnothing\), and \(D(1)=\operatorname{Spec}A\). These identities prove that the \(D(I)\) are precisely the opens of a topology and that the principal opens form a basis.',r'These are the standard elementary spectrum identities, checked in \citet[Lemma 26.5.1]{stacks2026foundations}, within the foundational theory already published in \citet{grothendieck1960ega1}. The displayed identities resolve both exercises.',refs=['grothendieck1960ega1','stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct',development='annotated')
item(184,'P000297','problem','The spectrum is a T0 space',r'The exercise asks for the weakest separation axiom: of two points, one can be separated from the other by a neighborhood.',r'For distinct prime ideals \(\mathfrak p,\mathfrak q\), choose an element in their symmetric difference. Its principal open contains exactly one of the two points. Thus \(\operatorname{Spec}A\) is \(T_0\); it is generally neither \(T_1\) nor Hausdorff.',r'This is an immediate consequence of the spectrum topology in \citet[Section 26.5]{stacks2026foundations}. It is a classical exercise, and no stronger separation property is inferred.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct',development='annotated')
item(184,'P000297','construction','Localization and the equality of fractions',r'The localization uses a multiplicative set \(S\) excluding zero and identifies \(a/s\) and \(a^{\prime}/s^{\prime}\) when some \(s^{\prime\prime}\in S\) kills \(s^{\prime}a-sa^{\prime}\). The exercise asks for equivalence of this relation.',r'For a multiplicative set \(S\subset A\) containing \(1\), the relation \((a,s)\sim(a^{\prime},s^{\prime})\) iff \(t(s^{\prime}a-sa^{\prime})=0\) for some \(t\in S\) is reflexive, symmetric and transitive; fraction addition and multiplication make the quotient \(S^{-1}A\). If \(0\in S\), this is the zero ring. For modules the same formula replaces \(a\) by a module element.',r'This is the standard localization construction used throughout \citet{grothendieck1960ega1} and \citet[Section 10.9]{stacks2026foundations}. Allowing the zero ring is necessary to include \(A_f\) when \(f\) is nilpotent and its principal open is empty.',refs=['grothendieck1960ega1','stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The conventional condition that 1 belongs to S, and the harmless zero-ring case needed by the next construction, are supplied.')
item(184,'P000297','construction','The structure sheaf from localizations',r'The structure sheaf is specified by \(\Gamma(U_f,\mathcal O)=A_{\{f^n\}}\).',r'There is a sheaf of rings \(\mathcal O_X\) on \(X=\operatorname{Spec}A\) with \(\Gamma(D(f),\mathcal O_X)=A_f\), compatible with localization restriction maps. Its stalk at \(\mathfrak p\) is \(A_{\mathfrak p}\), so it is a sheaf of local rings at points, and \(\Gamma(X,\mathcal O_X)=A\).',r'The result is foundational scheme theory; the explicit construction and section and stalk identifications are checked in \citet[Lemma 26.5.4]{stacks2026foundations}.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','grothendieck1960ega1'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(184,'P000297','definition','The sheaf equalizer and its two restriction arrows',r'The diagram uses circled-plus signs, but its caption says product of sets. The question asks why there are two arrows to the sections on intersections.',r'A presheaf \(F\) of sets is a sheaf if, for every open cover \(U=\bigcup U_\alpha\), the map \(F(U)\to\prod_\alpha F(U_\alpha)\) identifies \(F(U)\) with the equalizer of two maps to \(\prod_{\alpha,\beta}F(U_\alpha\cap U_\beta)\). Their \((\alpha,\beta)\)-components restrict \(s_\alpha\) and \(s_\beta\), respectively; the target open is the same, but the input component differs.',r'This is the standard gluing and uniqueness axiom, checked in \citet[Section 6.7]{stacks2026foundations}. Products, not direct sums, permit arbitrary compatible local families. The written caption supports reading the symbol as an informal product notation.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The product meaning follows the prose caption; the overwritten source index in the second arrow is not treated as a secure alpha.',development='annotated')
item(184,'P000297','problem','Stalks and canonical sheafification',r'After mentioning stalks, the exercise asks for a unique sheaf with the same stalks as any presheaf.',r'For a presheaf \(F\) of sets or abelian groups, there is a sheaf \(aF\) and a canonical morphism \(F\to aF\) inducing an isomorphism on every stalk. It is characterized by \(\operatorname{Hom}(aF,G)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}(F,G)\) for each sheaf \(G\), and is unique up to unique isomorphism compatible with this morphism. Merely prescribing abstract stalks without the specialization and local-section data does not determine a sheaf.',r'Canonical sheafification is classical; \citet[Lemmas 6.17.1--6.17.2]{stacks2026foundations} gives the construction and universal property. The seminar had already recorded it in 1968-152.4; it recurs below in 1970-187.7.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Uniqueness is relative to the canonical map or universal property, not an assertion about a bare collection of stalks.')
item(184,'P000297','method','Constructing a sheaf from a basis',r'The remark says that to define a sheaf it is enough to know a presheaf on a basis.',r'A sheaf is uniquely determined by its values and restrictions on a topological basis. Conversely, basis data extend to a sheaf with those basis values when they satisfy the sheaf gluing and uniqueness conditions for covers of basis opens by basis opens. An arbitrary presheaf on a basis can be sheafified, but its original values need not be preserved.',r'The precise construction is \citet[Section 6.30]{stacks2026foundations}; it is the established method used in the structure-sheaf construction. The extra basis sheaf condition is essential to the literal converse.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(184,'P000297-P000298','theorem','Principal-open inclusions, radicals and restriction maps',r'The notes write \(U_f\subset U_g\) iff a power of \(f\) is divisible by \(g\), refer to the intersection of prime ideals, and then give the induced fraction map.',r'For \(f,g\in A\), \(D(f)\subseteq D(g)\) iff \(f\in\sqrt{(g)}\), equivalently \(f^m=gh\) for some \(m\geq1\), \(h\in A\). Then the restriction \(A_g\to A_f\) sends \(a/g^k\) to \(ah^k/f^{mk}\). The algebraic fact is \(\sqrt I=\bigcap_{\mathfrak p\supseteq I}\mathfrak p\); the intersection of all primes is the nilradical \(\sqrt{(0)}\).',r'These are standard localization and radical identities, checked in \citet[Lemma 26.5.1 and construction following it]{stacks2026foundations}. The mismatched quantifier and exponent in the source are normalized to a single integer.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The radical is explicitly the radical of an ideal; the unqualified intersection of all prime ideals means the nilradical.')
item(184,'P000298','definition','Local affine models and the older word prescheme',r'A prescheme is defined as a ringed space locally isomorphic to an affine scheme.',r'In current terminology a scheme is a locally ringed space admitting an open cover by affine schemes. The older word prescheme used here imposes no separatedness; the notebook reserves scheme for the later diagonal-closed condition.',r'This is historical terminology from the early EGA framework \citep{grothendieck1960ega1}. The modern definition is checked in \citet[Section 26.5]{stacks2026foundations}. The change is terminological, not a defect in the underlying construction.',refs=['grothendieck1960ega1','stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct')
item(184,'P000298','problem','Principal opens are affine schemes',r'The exercise asks that \((U_f,\mathcal O)\) be affine for every \(f\in A\).',r'The canonical map \(\operatorname{Spec}A_f\to\operatorname{Spec}A\) is an isomorphism of locally ringed spaces onto \(D(f)\) equipped with the restricted structure sheaf. In particular this also covers the empty scheme when \(A_f=0\).',r'This is part of the basic affine-spectrum theorem, checked in \citet[Lemma 26.5.4]{stacks2026foundations}. It supplies the local compatibility required in the definition of a scheme.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct')
item(184,'P000298','construction','Affine fibre products and tensor products of rings',r'The product is written \(\operatorname{Spec}A\times\operatorname{Spec}B=\operatorname{Spec}(A\otimes B)\), followed by an exercise on general preschemes.',r'For commutative \(R\)-algebras \(A,B\), the fibre product over \(\operatorname{Spec}R\) is \(\operatorname{Spec}A\times_{\operatorname{Spec}R}\operatorname{Spec}B\simeq\operatorname{Spec}(A\otimes_RB)\). It satisfies the categorical fibre-product universal property. An unqualified product of schemes is over \(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z\).',r'The affine formula and universal property are checked in \citet[Lemma 26.17.2]{stacks2026foundations}; they are foundational scheme theory already available in \citet{grothendieck1960ega1}. The base ring cannot be omitted when working over a fixed field or base scheme.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','grothendieck1960ega1'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(184,'P000298','claim','The empty product of the two residue-field spectra',r'The boxed example says \(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z_2\times\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z_3\) is empty.',r'Interpreting \(\mathbb Z_2=\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z\) and \(\mathbb Z_3=\mathbb Z/3\mathbb Z\), the product over \(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z\) is empty: the tensor product of the rings is zero, because both 2 and 3 annihilate it and \(3-2=1\). This is not the Cartesian product of the underlying one-point sets.',r'The statement follows directly from the affine fibre-product formula \citep[Lemma 26.17.2]{stacks2026foundations}. The source notation is read as residue fields, not as rings of 2-adic and 3-adic integers.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',reconstruction='The interpretation of the two subscripted rings and the base are made explicit.')
item(184,'P000298','method','Kirillov: gluing products from affine charts',r'The framed Kirillov intervention covers \(X,Y\) by affine opens, forms their products, and refines their overlaps; its lemma says products of open inclusions are open inclusions.',r'Given schemes \(X,Y\) over \(S\), choose affine covers over affine opens of \(S\). The affine products \(U_\alpha\times_S V_\beta\) glue along their canonical overlap products. Base change preserves open immersions, so these pieces form an open cover of \(X\times_SY\), and the affine universal properties glue uniquely to the global one. Both cover indices range independently.',r'The precise gluing statement and open-immersion property are \citet[Lemmas 26.17.3--26.17.4 and 26.17.6]{stacks2026foundations}. This explains the improvisation without interpreting the illegible union subscript as a new mathematical restriction.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',speaker='A. A. Kirillov')
item(184,'P000298','definition','Separatedness and the affine diagonal exercise',r'A prescheme is called a scheme if its diagonal in the product is closed; the exercise asks that an affine scheme satisfy this condition.',r'A morphism \(X\to S\) is separated when \(\Delta_{X/S}:X\to X\times_SX\) is a closed immersion. Every affine morphism is separated: for \(X=\operatorname{Spec}A\) over \(\operatorname{Spec}R\), the diagonal is induced by the surjective multiplication map \(A\otimes_RA\to A\). For a scheme over \(S\), its diagonal is an immersion, so closed image is an equivalent criterion.',r'The modern definition and affine proof are \citet[Lemmas 26.21.1--26.21.2 and Definition 26.21.3]{stacks2026foundations}. The lecture uses an older naming convention in which separated schemes alone are called schemes.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','grothendieck1960ega1'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(184,'P000298-P000299','definition','Quasi-coherent modules by local free presentations',r'A sheaf of \(\mathcal O_X\)-modules is quasi-coherent if locally it is a cokernel \(\mathcal O^{(S)}\to\mathcal O^{(T)}\), with arbitrary sets of summands.',r'On a scheme \(X\), an \(\mathcal O_X\)-module \(\mathcal F\) is quasi-coherent iff locally it admits an exact presentation \(\mathcal O_U^{(S)}\to\mathcal O_U^{(T)}\to\mathcal F|_U\to0\), where the free sheaves are direct sums indexed by arbitrary sets. On an affine open \(U=\operatorname{Spec}A\), this is equivalent to \(\mathcal F|_U\simeq\widetilde M\) for an \(A\)-module.',r'The equivalence is checked in \citet[Lemma 26.7.4 and Section 17.10]{stacks2026foundations}. No finiteness of the index sets is part of quasi-coherence itself.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','grothendieck1960ega1'],literal='correct')
item(184,'P000299','problem','An example of a non-quasi-coherent module sheaf',r'The exercise asks for a sheaf that is not quasi-coherent.',r'Let \(X=\operatorname{Spec}k[t]\), \(U=D(t)\), and \(j:U\hookrightarrow X\). Extension by zero \(j_!\mathcal O_U\), considered as an \(\mathcal O_X\)-module, is nonzero but has no nonzero global section: a nonzero regular function on the irreducible open \(U\) has support all of \(U\), whose closure contains the missing origin. The affine equivalence would force any quasi-coherent sheaf with zero global module to vanish. Thus \(j_!\mathcal O_U\) is a counterexample.',r'The exercise is classical; the supplied example is a direct application of extension by zero \citep[Section 17.3]{stacks2026foundations} and the affine equivalence \citep[Lemma 26.7.5]{stacks2026foundations}. It is an explanatory solution constructed here, not a new research result.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct',development='annotated',reconstruction='The notebook contains only the question; the explicit affine-line example and verification are supplied by the companion.')
item(184,'P000299','theorem','The affine module--sheaf equivalence',r'The notes call \(\mathcal F\mapsto\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec}A,\mathcal F)\) an isomorphism from quasi-coherent sheaves to modules, and say global sections determine the sheaf.',r'For a commutative ring \(A\), the functors \(M\mapsto\widetilde M\) and \(\mathcal F\mapsto\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec}A,\mathcal F)\) are mutually quasi-inverse equivalences between \(A\)-modules and quasi-coherent \(\mathcal O_{\operatorname{Spec}A}\)-modules. The identifications \(M\simeq\Gamma(\widetilde M)\) and \(\widetilde{\Gamma(\mathcal F)}\simeq\mathcal F\) are natural; literal equality of the categories is unnecessary.',r'This is standard affine scheme theory, checked in \citet[Lemma 26.7.5]{stacks2026foundations}, with antecedents in \citet{serre1955coherent} and its general-ring formulation in \citet{grothendieck1960ega1}. It is developed again in record 190.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','serre1955coherent','grothendieck1960ega1'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The word isomorphism is interpreted as categorical equivalence with the canonical natural isomorphisms.')
item(184,'P000299','theorem','Vanishing of positive-degree quasi-coherent cohomology on affines',r'The separate parenthesis says that for affine spaces the cohomology is zero, without degrees or coefficients.',r'If \(X=\operatorname{Spec}A\) and \(\mathcal F\) is quasi-coherent, then \(H^q(X,\mathcal F)=0\) for every \(q>0\). Degree zero is the generally nonzero module of global sections. The assertion concerns affine schemes and quasi-coherent coefficients, not arbitrary sheaves on an affine topological space.',r'The classical affine acyclicity theorem is checked in \citet[Lemma 30.2.2]{stacks2026foundations}, following \citet{serre1955coherent} and \citet{grothendieck1957homological}. Exactness of global sections on quasi-coherent modules is related to the affine equivalence, but the notebook\textquotesingle s unqualified equivalence phrase is not itself a proof of sheaf-cohomological vanishing.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','serre1955coherent','grothendieck1957homological'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='Positive degree and quasi-coherent coefficients are indispensable qualifications.')
correction('P000297','sheaf equalizer symbols',r'\(\bigoplus\) with the caption product of sets',r'Interpret the two symbols as \(\prod\), not direct sums','The scan visibly has circled-plus signs but explicitly labels the first a product of sets. An arbitrary sheaf cover requires products; the source meaning is clearer than its notation.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000297','localization applied to all principal opens','S is required not to contain zero, followed by S={f^n} for every f','Allow the zero-ring localization when f is nilpotent','The empty principal open has zero-ring sections; the preceding restricted localization definition does not cover this case.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000297','uniqueness of a sheaf with prescribed stalks','a sheaf with the same stalks, and unique','the canonical sheafification with its stalk-isomorphism map, unique by its universal property','Abstract stalks alone do not determine a sheaf; the classical exercise concerns canonical sheafification.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000298','prescheme and scheme terminology','prescheme; scheme when the diagonal is closed','modern scheme; separated scheme when the diagonal is closed','This is the early EGA convention used consistently by the lecture; it is a terminology note rather than an error in transcription.','both','provenance')
correction('P000299','affine cohomology parenthesis','for affine spaces cohomology is zero','for affine schemes and quasi-coherent coefficients, cohomology in strictly positive degrees is zero','The scan has no degree or coefficient qualification. Degree zero consists of global sections and is generally nonzero.','both','faithful_error')
