talk(187,'1970-09-28','D. A. Kazhdan','P000299-P000301','Sheaf images, sheafification and cohomology',r'The lecture constructs direct and inverse images of sheaves, using the adjunction to characterize inverse image and a two-point example to explain sheafification. It then recalls the abelian category of sheaves, enough injectives, derived sections and the use of acyclic covers. The sign saying that the next meeting is skipped does not supply a separate date for the following cohomology reminder; its placement within this record is retained with that qualification.')
item(187,'P000299','construction','Direct image of a sheaf',r'For \(f:X\to Y\), the notes define \(\Gamma(V,f_*\mathcal F)=\Gamma(f^{-1}V,\mathcal F)\) and ask to check the sheaf axiom. For a one-point target they recover global sections.',r'If \(f:X\to Y\) is continuous and \(\mathcal F\) a sheaf of sets or abelian groups, then \((f_*\mathcal F)(V)=\mathcal F(f^{-1}V)\), with inherited restrictions, is a sheaf. Inverse images of an open cover cover its inverse image, so the gluing axiom is inherited. For \(Y\) a point its value is \(\Gamma(X,\mathcal F)\).',r'This is the classical direct-image functor, checked in \citet[Lemma 6.21.1]{stacks2026foundations}; the verification is the elementary exercise posed in the notebook.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(187,'P000299','claim','Sections on the empty open set',r'The parenthesis says that when \(f^{-1}(V)\) is empty, its set of sections is empty.',r'For a sheaf of sets, the section set on the empty open is a singleton, by the sheaf axiom for the empty cover. For a sheaf of abelian groups it is the zero group, whose underlying set has one element. Accordingly, \((f_*\mathcal F)(V)\) is terminal, not an empty set, when \(f^{-1}(V)=\varnothing\).',r'The correction follows immediately from the sheaf definition \citep[Section 6.7]{stacks2026foundations}. The scan repeats the word empty, so this is a faithful mathematical error rather than a translation error.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(187,'P000299-P000300','definition','Inverse image characterized by adjunction',r'The source writes \(f^*\mathcal G\) and a natural bijection \(\operatorname{Hom}(f^*\mathcal G,\mathcal F)=\operatorname{Hom}(\mathcal G,f_*\mathcal F)\).',r'For continuous \(f:X\to Y\), the inverse-image functor on sheaves of sets or abelian groups, denoted \(f^{-1}\), is left adjoint to \(f_*\): \(\operatorname{Hom}_X(f^{-1}\mathcal G,\mathcal F)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}_Y(\mathcal G,f_*\mathcal F)\), naturally in both arguments. This determines inverse image up to canonical isomorphism. For modules on ringed spaces, the usual \(f^*\) additionally tensors with \(\mathcal O_X\).',r'The adjunction is checked in \citet[Section 6.21]{stacks2026foundations}. The distinction between sheaf inverse image and module pullback prevents transferring the bare topological formula to a different category.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Modern notation f inverse is used for the functor denoted f star in this topological discussion.')
item(187,'P000300','construction','The inverse-image presheaf and its sheafification',r'The construction uses the direct limit of \(\mathcal G(V)\) over neighborhoods \(V\supset f(U)\), and asks to show that its associated sheaf is inverse image.',r'For open \(U\subseteq X\), define \(P(U)=\varinjlim_{V\supseteq f(U)}\mathcal G(V)\), where \(V\) runs through open subsets of \(Y\), ordered by reverse inclusion. Restrictions make \(P\) a presheaf, and \(f^{-1}\mathcal G=aP\). Its stalk at \(x\) is canonically \(\mathcal G_{f(x)}\); sheafification is essential for the adjunction on sheaves.',r'The construction and stalk statement are \citet[Lemmas 6.21.3--6.21.4 and the following definition]{stacks2026foundations}. The source formula is correct once the open-neighborhood indexing and associated sheaf are retained.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(187,'P000300','claim','Why the two-point pullback presheaf is not a sheaf',r'Two isolated points map to one point. The construction gives the same value \(\mathcal G\) on each singleton and on the whole two-point space, which the source labels not a sheaf.',r'Let \(X=\{x_1,x_2\}\) be discrete, \(Y=\{y\}\), and let the sheaf on \(Y\) have value a set \(A\) with at least two elements. The inverse-image presheaf has value \(A\) on every nonempty open, but its value on \(X\) cannot glue arbitrary independent choices at the two points. Its sheafification has \(A\times A\) on \(X\), the locally constant sheaf with fibre \(A\). The example is trivial for a singleton \(A\).',r'This is the standard distinction between a constant presheaf and constant sheaf, checked in \citet[Example 6.17.4]{stacks2026foundations}. It explains the notebook\textquotesingle s emphatic need for sheafification.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(187,'P000300','definition','Morphisms of sheaves as natural transformations',r'The footnote defines a morphism by maps on the section sets for every open, commuting with restrictions. One arrow is overwritten and resembles a two-headed arrow.',r'A morphism \(\phi:\mathcal F\to\mathcal G\) over \(X\) is a family \(\phi_U:\mathcal F(U)\to\mathcal G(U)\) such that restriction and \(\phi\) commute for every inclusion of opens. In the group or module category, these are group or module homomorphisms. They need not be bijections; isomorphisms are those with an inverse natural transformation.',r'This is the standard presheaf and sheaf morphism definition \citep[Sections 6.2 and 6.7]{stacks2026foundations}. The clear prose and separate right-pointing arrow determine the intended general morphism; the overwritten arrow does not impose invertibility.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(187,'P000301','theorem','The universal property of the associated sheaf',r'The corrected Hom display is followed by a canonical map from the presheaf to its associated object, but the word presheaf is mistakenly repeated for both objects.',r'For a presheaf \(P\), sheafification is a sheaf \(aP\) with a morphism \(P\to aP\), such that every presheaf morphism \(P\to G\) into a sheaf factors uniquely through \(aP\). Equivalently, \(\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathrm{Sh}}(aP,G)\simeq\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathrm{PSh}}(P,G)\) naturally. This makes sheafification left adjoint to inclusion of sheaves into presheaves.',r'The exact statement is \citet[Lemma 6.17.3]{stacks2026foundations}; the explicit canonical map in this occurrence supplies the qualification omitted in the earlier same-stalks exercise, 1970-184.7.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The second object is a sheaf, as required by the heading and Hom universal property; the cancelled preliminary formula is not used.')
item(187,'P000301','theorem','Sheaves of abelian groups form an abelian category',r'Lemma 1 says that the category of sheaves of abelian groups on \(X\) is abelian.',r'For every topological space \(X\), the category \(\operatorname{Sh}(X,\mathbf{Ab})\) is abelian. Kernels are computed on each open, whereas cokernels are obtained by sheafifying the presheaf cokernel; a sequence is exact iff its sequences of stalks are exact. Sections on an arbitrary open need not preserve surjectivity.',r'This is a basic theorem of sheaf homological algebra, established in the framework of \citet{grothendieck1957homological} and checked in \citet[Section 18.1]{stacks2026foundations}. It is an expository lemma, not a research claim of 1970.',refs=['grothendieck1957homological','stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct')
item(187,'P000301','theorem','Enough injective sheaves',r'Lemma 2 says that every sheaf embeds in an injective sheaf.',r'For every topological space \(X\), the abelian category of sheaves of abelian groups has enough injectives. Thus every sheaf admits an injective resolution. One construction embeds each stalk into an injective abelian group and maps the sheaf into the product of the corresponding direct-image sheaves at the points.',r'This is the classical existence theorem used by \citet{grothendieck1957homological}; the pointwise construction is checked in \citet[Section 19.4]{stacks2026foundations}. The claim is about abelian-group sheaves, not an unqualified assertion for sheaves of sets.',refs=['grothendieck1957homological','stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(187,'P000301','definition','Cohomology as derived global sections',r'The notes call \(\Gamma\) left semiexact and define \(H^i\) as its derived functors, contrasting them with Cech calculations.',r'Global sections \(\Gamma(X,-):\operatorname{Sh}(X,\mathbf{Ab})\to\mathbf{Ab}\) are left exact. For an injective resolution \(\mathcal F\to I^\bullet\), define \(H^q(X,\mathcal F)=H^q(\Gamma(X,I^\bullet))=R^q\Gamma(X,\mathcal F)\). The resulting groups are independent of the resolution up to canonical isomorphism and have the usual long exact sequence.',r'This is the derived-functor definition of sheaf cohomology from \citet{grothendieck1957homological}; its precise formulation is checked in \citet[Section 20.2]{stacks2026foundations}. The notebook correctly separates the abstract definition from methods of computing it.',refs=['grothendieck1957homological','stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct')
item(187,'P000301','theorem','Cech cohomology on acyclic covers',r'The final lines mention a topology basis with vanishing Cech groups and say that actual cohomology agrees with Cech computations on acyclic covers.',r'Let \(\mathcal U=\{U_\alpha\}\) cover \(X\), and let \(\mathcal F\) be an abelian sheaf. If every nonempty finite intersection \(U_{\alpha_0}\cap\cdots\cap U_{\alpha_p}\) is \(\mathcal F\)-acyclic in positive derived-functor degrees, then \(\check H^q(\mathcal U,\mathcal F)\simeq H^q(X,\mathcal F)\). A basis criterion using Cech vanishing must in addition specify covers cofinal under refinement and stability of their intersections; one unspecified vanishing display is insufficient.',r'The precise cover comparison and basis criterion are \citet[Section 20.11, especially Lemmas 20.11.6 and 20.11.9]{stacks2026foundations}. For quasi-coherent sheaves, affine intersections give the familiar application \citep[Lemma 30.2.6]{stacks2026foundations}, building on \citet{grothendieck1957homological}.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','grothendieck1957homological'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The stated acyclic-cover theorem is a precise sufficient interpretation; the notebook does not record every assumption of its separate basis criterion.')
correction('P000299','empty inverse-image parenthesis','the inverse image may be empty, then the set of sections is empty','sections on the empty open form a singleton set, or the zero group','Both occurrences of empty are visible in the Russian scan. The sheaf axiom for the empty cover determines a terminal section object.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000300','inverse-image functor notation',r'\(f^*\) for sheaves on topological spaces',r'Modern \(f^{-1}\); reserve module pullback \(f^*\) for tensoring with the structure sheaf','The source discusses sets or abelian groups and uses the sheaf adjunction; this is a notation clarification, not a transcription error.','both','provenance')
correction('P000301','associated object after Hom display','F is the associated presheaf','F is the associated sheaf','The scan repeats predpuchok, but the heading, canonical map and universal property identify sheafification.','both','faithful_error')
