talk(188,'1970-10-05','I. M. Gelfand','P000302','An incomplete question about second-order elliptic systems',r'The short note asks about solutions of a second-order elliptic system on a manifold, explicitly without lower-order terms. It then mentions the Laplace operator, counting solutions modulo two, topology and the Dirichlet problem. No operator, equation, boundary data or intended notion of nontrivial solution is specified, so the passage does not identify a definite modern open problem. The date and speaker come from the catalogue; the leaf itself has no date before this fragment.')
item(188,'P000302','question','Solvability of an unspecified elliptic system',r'The source says \ru{Эллипт. сист. 2-го порядка} on a manifold \ru{без младших членов}, then asks whether it has solutions.',r'A determinate solvability question requires the operator or nonlinear system, the domain and boundary conditions, the right-hand side, the solution class and the exclusion or inclusion of trivial solutions. Absence of lower-order terms does not supply these missing data. For a homogeneous linear system, zero is always a solution.',r'Targeted English and Russian searches combining Gelfand, elliptic systems without lower-order terms and parity of solutions did not yield an exact publication match. The fragment is not equated with the Atiyah--Singer index problem or with the unrelated scalar exponential equation often called the Gelfand problem.',cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',status='As of 9 November 2026: the mathematical question is underdetermined; no solved or open status is assigned.',confidence='low')
item(188,'P000302','claim','The unexplained Laplace comparison',r'The next sentence says that for the Laplace operator it is known that there are no solutions.',r'Without the omitted qualifier, this is not a valid general statement about harmonic functions: constants solve the homogeneous scalar Laplace equation, and zero solves homogeneous Dirichlet data. The source may intend a nontriviality or boundary requirement, but it supplies none that can be reconstructed uniquely.',r'The Laplace sentence cannot be identified with a particular nonexistence theorem from the available context. Its failure as a bare statement does not license attributing a specific false theorem to the speaker.',cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',status='As of 9 November 2026: an essential condition is missing; the intended nonexistence assertion remains unidentified.',confidence='low')
item(188,'P000302','question','Parity of solutions and an unspecified topological principle',r'The remaining lines mention the number of solutions modulo two, that in principle the maximum is topology, and a zero-th solution of the Dirichlet problem.',r'To define a parity count one needs a finite or suitably compact zero-dimensional solution set, or an appropriate proper Fredholm or finite-dimensional degree construction, with regularity or multiplicity conventions. None is stated. The words topology and Dirichlet do not determine such an invariant or establish its stability.',r'The same topic searches found general index and degree-theoretic surroundings but no exact statement matching these lines. These are preserved as an unidentified research direction; no numerical invariant, proposed theorem or new open problem is manufactured from the fragment.',cls='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',status='As of 9 November 2026: neither a parity theorem nor a well-posed parity problem can be extracted without additional archival information.',confidence='low')
correction('P000302','elliptic-system qualifier','on a manifold without lower-order terms','the system has no lower-order terms; the source does not say without boundary','The Russian text places bez before mladshikh chlenov. This prevents a tempting but unsupported reconstruction as a closed-manifold problem.','both','ambiguity')
