talk(189,'1970-10-05','D. A. Kazhdan','P000302','Regular elements and the adjoint quotient',r'The fragment calls itself a report on one particular work but names no author. It discusses regular and subregular elements of a complex semisimple group and the quotient by conjugation. The centralizer numbers and the use of rank require correction. Steinberg\textquotesingle s 1965 theorem matches the identifiable quotient criterion under its hypotheses; the notebook does not state a theorem on ADE surface singularities or transverse slices.')
item(189,'P000302','definition','Regularity is minimal centralizer dimension',r'A regular element is said to have centralizer of rank \(n-1\); the group is described as complex semisimple, of type \(A_n\) or \(D_n\).',r'For a connected complex semisimple algebraic group of rank \(r\), an element is regular when \(\dim Z_G(g)=r\), the minimal possible centralizer dimension. It is dimension, not rank of the centralizer, that detects regularity. In \(SL_n\), \(r=n-1\), whereas a group of type \(A_n\) has rank \(n\); the notebook leaves its integer convention unresolved.',r'This is the definition in \citet[p.~49]{steinberg1965regular}, published in 1965. The mismatch of centralizer rank and dimension is a faithful source error; replacing the ambiguous n by rank r makes the assertion precise.',refs=['steinberg1965regular'],literal='false as written',reconstruction='The group rank r replaces the unspecified matrix-size or Dynkin-index convention.')
item(189,'P000302','definition','Subregular centralizers have dimension rank plus two',r'The source assigns a subregular element centralizer dimension \(n-2\) and adds two coinciding eigenvalues.',r'In the usual complex semisimple setting, subregular elements have centralizer dimension \(r+2\), the next dimension after regularity. For a semisimple element of \(SL_n\) with exactly one eigenvalue of multiplicity two and all other eigenvalues distinct, the centralizer dimension is \(4+(n-2)-1=n+1=(n-1)+2\). This eigenvalue description is a type-A example, not a uniform definition for type D.',r'The minimum-dimension characterization and structure of irregular elements are part of the classical setting of \citet[Sections 1 and 5]{steinberg1965regular}. The explicit block-centralizer calculation verifies the type-A correction directly; no later singularity theorem is inserted into the fragment.',refs=['steinberg1965regular'],literal='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',reconstruction='The standard subregular dimension and the checked type-A example are given separately; the archival n minus two is not retained as a valid centralizer formula.')
item(189,'P000302','theorem','Regularity and smoothness of the adjoint quotient',r'The map \(G\to H/W\), with \(H\) a Cartan subgroup and \(W\) its Weyl group, is followed by regular element if and only if the map is regular there.',r'For a connected simply connected complex semisimple group \(G\) of rank \(r\), the adjoint quotient \(\chi:G\to H/W\simeq\mathbb A^r\) is smooth at \(g\) iff \(g\) is regular. Equivalently the differentials of its fundamental character coordinates are independent there. Here regular for the map must mean nonsingular or submersive, not merely algebraic regularity, which holds everywhere.',r'\citet[Theorem 1.5, Theorem 8.1 and Remark 8.7]{steinberg1965regular} supplies the exact criterion and its simply connected qualification. This verifies the identifiable statement, but does not prove that Steinberg is the unnamed particular work reported in 1970; no stronger bibliographical identification is made.',refs=['steinberg1965regular'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',reconstruction='Simply connectedness and the smooth/submersive meaning of regular map are explicit sufficient hypotheses and terminology.')
correction('P000302','regular centralizer condition','centralizer rank n minus 1','centralizer dimension equals rank G','The scan distinguishes rang from dim; the correct regularity condition is dimension, and the role of n is not specified. Steinberg 1965, p. 49.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000302','subregular centralizer condition','dimension n minus 2','dimension rank G plus 2 in the usual subregular setting','For the stated two-equal-eigenvalues example in SL_n, a block calculation gives n plus 1. The source n minus 2 cannot be the centralizer dimension.','both','faithful_error')
checkpoint()

talk(190,'1970-10-12','D. A. Kazhdan','P000303-P000304','Quasi-coherent sheaves and their global modules on affines',r'Kazhdan returns to the module sheaf, the equivalence of local definitions of quasi-coherence and recovery from global sections. The proof uses finite principal-open covers and the canonical localization map. The finite exponents in the presentation diagram need arbitrary index sets in the general quasi-coherent case. The record continues only through the localization-map paragraph at the top of P000304, ending before the Thompson heading; the Rota material at the bottom of P000302 belongs to a separate catalogue record.')
item(190,'P000303','construction','The sheaf associated to a module',r'The notes recall module localization by fractions and set \(\Gamma(U_f,\mathcal F_M)=M_f\); listeners are asked to verify that this is a sheaf.',r'For a commutative ring \(A\) and any \(A\)-module \(M\), the localization rule \(D(f)\mapsto M_f\), with canonical restrictions, satisfies the basis sheaf condition and determines a unique sheaf \(\widetilde M\). Its stalk at \(\mathfrak p\) is \(M_{\mathfrak p}\), and its global sections are \(M\). Fractions satisfy \(m/s=m^{\prime}/s^{\prime}\) iff some \(t\in S\) kills \(s^{\prime}m-sm^{\prime}\).',r'The construction is the established affine module-sheaf construction, checked in \citet[Lemma 26.5.4 and Lemma 26.7.1]{stacks2026foundations}. It extends the localization material of 1970-184.4--5 to arbitrary modules.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','grothendieck1960ega1'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(190,'P000303','problem','Equivalent definitions of quasi-coherence',r'The initial definition uses local cokernels of free sheaves; the second uses local isomorphisms to \(\mathcal F_M\). An exercise asks for equivalence.',r'For a sheaf of modules on a scheme, the following local conditions are equivalent: it is associated to a module on an affine open neighborhood; it admits a presentation there by direct sums of copies of the structure sheaf. Arbitrary modules admit free presentations, and localization is exact, so a module presentation sheafifies to a local free presentation; conversely an affine free-sheaf map comes from the corresponding module map.',r'This is \citet[Lemma 26.7.4]{stacks2026foundations}, already implicit in \citet{grothendieck1960ega1}. The equivalence is an expository exercise, not a new problem of 1970.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','grothendieck1960ega1'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(190,'P000303','method','Free presentations need not have finite rank',r'The proposed solution displays \(A^m\to A^n\to M\) and \(\mathcal O^m\to\mathcal O^n\to\mathcal F_M\), then reverses the construction by taking sections.',r'For arbitrary \(M\), use an exact sequence \(A^{(I)}\to A^{(J)}\to M\to0\) with possibly infinite sets \(I,J\), and the corresponding sheaf sequence. Finite \(m,n\) are justified when \(M\) is finitely presented, a stronger assumption than quasi-coherence. In the converse, form the cokernel of the module map and identify its sheaf locally; do not assume right exactness of arbitrary section functors as a premise.',r'Exactness of the associated-sheaf functor and the free-presentation characterization are checked in \citet[Lemmas 26.7.1--26.7.4]{stacks2026foundations}. The arbitrary-index convention had been stated explicitly in the 7 September lecture but is suppressed in this diagram.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',reconstruction='The letters m and n are replaced by index sets for the unrestricted theorem. The proof order avoids using the desired affine exactness circularly.')
item(190,'P000303','theorem','Every quasi-coherent sheaf on an affine is a module sheaf',r'The main theorem says every quasi-coherent \(\mathcal F\) on \(\operatorname{Spec}A\) has the form \(\mathcal F_M\); its proof sets \(M=\Gamma(X,\mathcal F)\).',r'For \(X=\operatorname{Spec}A\) and quasi-coherent \(\mathcal F\), the canonical morphism \(\widetilde{\Gamma(X,\mathcal F)}\to\mathcal F\) is an isomorphism. Thus the local module presentations globalize uniquely up to the canonical module identification; no finite-generation, Noetherian or separatedness hypothesis beyond affineness is required.',r'This is the affine equivalence of \citet[Lemma 26.7.5]{stacks2026foundations}, first introduced in record 184 and classical by \citet{grothendieck1960ega1}. The choice of the global-section module is part of the theorem, rather than an arbitrary existence assertion.',refs=['stacks2026foundations','grothendieck1960ega1'],literal='correct')
item(190,'P000303','theorem','Quasi-compactness of affine spectra',r'The proof recalls that every affine scheme is quasi-compact, with a parenthesis about absence of separation, and chooses a finite subcover.',r'Every open cover of \(\operatorname{Spec}A\) has a finite subcover. Refine to principal opens \(D(f_i)\); covering means the \(f_i\) generate the unit ideal, so a finite sum \(\sum a_if_i=1\) selects a finite subcover. This topological quasi-compactness does not assert that the underlying space is Hausdorff.',r'This is checked in \citet[Lemma 26.5.1]{stacks2026foundations}. The parenthesis distinguishes quasi-compactness from a Hausdorff compactness convention; it does not deny the scheme-theoretic separatedness of affine schemes.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct',development='annotated')
item(190,'P000303-P000304','method','The canonical localization map and clearing denominators',r'After choosing a finite cover with \(\mathcal F|_{D(f_i)}=\widetilde M_i\), the notes construct \(M_f\to\Gamma(D(f),\mathcal F)\) because \(1/f\) is a section of the structure sheaf there.',r'For any module sheaf \(\mathcal F\), restriction \(M=\Gamma(X,\mathcal F)\to\Gamma(D(f),\mathcal F)\) factors canonically through \(M_f\), since multiplication by \(f\) is invertible on \(D(f)\). For quasi-coherent \(\mathcal F\) on affine \(X\), a finite principal-open cover with local module descriptions permits a common power of \(f\) to clear all denominators and compatibility defects; this proves the map is an isomorphism and hence the main theorem.',r'The localization identities and proof of global recovery are checked in \citet[Lemmas 26.7.1--26.7.5]{stacks2026foundations}. The construction of the map is valid before quasi-coherence; its being an isomorphism uses quasi-coherence and the finite cover.',refs=['stacks2026foundations'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The missing remainder of the proof is summarized using the standard denominator-clearing argument; the source itself stops after defining the natural map.')
correction('P000303','free presentation diagram',r'\(A^m\to A^n\to M\) with the same finite-looking exponents for sheaves',r'Use arbitrary direct-sum index sets, or assume M is finitely presented','The preceding lecture explicitly allowed arbitrary sets. Finite presentations do not characterize all quasi-coherent sheaves.','both','ambiguity')
